From: meenmachine1 <78238681+meenmachine1@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 21:41:13 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Decompiler: fail cleanly when a bitfield pull's reading op
 was already removed

BitFieldPullTransform::apply processes its PullRecords in order. Each
applyRecord may delete the op that defined the pulled value
(opDestroyRecursive on modOp once its output has no reads left). When a
later record's readOp is one of the ops deleted that way (an INT_AND in
the cases below), applyRecord works on a dead op: getSlot() doesn't find readVn
among its now-null inputs, returns numInput(), and opSetInput then reads
past the end of the input vector and dereferences garbage in
Varnode::eraseDescend. The decompiler segfaults.

Check that each record's readOp is still alive before applying it and throw
a LowlevelError otherwise, so the function fails with an error and the
process survives. Only functions that crashed before are affected.
(Skipping the record would likely also be correct, as the read no longer
exists, but that is left to the bitfield code's owners.)

Seen on: tcc 0.9.27 struct_decl, debug builds for x86-64 clang -O2,
AArch64 gcc -O2 and x86-64 Windows clang -O2 (DWARF/PDB types with
bitfields). Ghidra 12.1.4's decompiler process dies on all three.
Repro: decomp_dbg, restore the attached debug XML, load function
struct_decl, decompile.

diff --git a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/bitfield.cc b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/bitfield.cc
index 5d7d650..e369991 100644
--- a/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/bitfield.cc
+++ b/Ghidra/Features/Decompiler/src/decompile/cpp/bitfield.cc
@@ -1654,6 +1654,8 @@ void BitFieldPullTransform::apply(void)
   state.partialType = buildPartialType();
   while(!pullList.empty()) {
     PullRecord &rec(pullList.front());
+    if (rec.readOp != (PcodeOp *)0 && rec.readOp->isDead())
+      throw LowlevelError("Bitfield pull: op reading the field was removed by an earlier pull");
     if (rec.type == PullRecord::equal) {
       applyCompareRecord(rec);
     }
